WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.280
So there's going to be many times on this course that I take you through good principles for effective AI,

00:05.280 --> 00:11.759
agentic engineering. And we're going to start now with the sort of basic five big ideas for successful

00:11.759 --> 00:17.039
vibe coding. And it all comes under the heading, be the boss. The first one is one we've already

00:17.039 --> 00:23.359
talked about, which is about spending time on your agents at MD. You want it to be concise,

00:23.359 --> 00:26.399
and you want to cover importantly three different areas.

00:26.399 --> 00:29.440
You want to spec what needs to be done very clearly.

00:29.440 --> 00:32.799
You want to describe the style that it needs to be done in

00:32.799 --> 00:35.960
and you need to be explaining what it means

00:35.960 --> 00:39.039
to be successful so that it's absolutely clear

00:39.039 --> 00:41.159
how to gauge success at the end of it.

00:41.159 --> 00:45.560
That would be a good, concise, clear agent.md.

00:45.560 --> 00:50.759
You need to start your project simple, straightforward, easy. And I'm not

00:50.759 --> 00:55.000
just saying that in your agents.md, you begin the first section with something that's

00:55.000 --> 01:01.659
easy. I'm saying your entire project, your first agents.md, should itself just be the

01:01.659 --> 01:08.019
very basic MVP, but begin by building something basic in your whole agents.md. Go through

01:08.019 --> 01:11.480
that entire iteration, including testing at each

01:11.480 --> 01:16.519
point including sub steps make sure that it works and then decide to take it a

01:16.519 --> 01:21.000
step more complex. If you try and start by boiling the ocean with a super

01:21.000 --> 01:25.400
complicated application spelled out in your agents.md then you might go off

01:25.400 --> 01:30.719
track off the rails it might not work and you'll just get stuck. So start simple.

01:30.719 --> 01:34.560
And obviously that goes along with that is to then work incrementally,

01:34.560 --> 01:40.959
constantly testing, constantly validating the success criteria at each step. If you're working in

01:40.959 --> 01:46.719
this very regimented way, this disciplined way, then you can guarantee at each point that you've

01:46.719 --> 01:57.040
reached that milestone successfully, and you can always go back if you haven't. And then, you know, these all sort of belong together, but don't get lazy,

01:57.040 --> 02:02.239
avoid the trap, especially when you've had some early success with vibe coding. It's gone off

02:02.239 --> 02:06.000
and built you a first person shooter. It's gone off and built you a camban and you're thinking,

02:06.000 --> 02:10.800
oh, wow, this just works. I'll just yolo. I'll just set it off. I'll come back later.

02:10.800 --> 02:18.240
And then I'll just keep pushing ahead. And it's so tempting. It is so easy to get lazy to start to take it for granted and

02:18.240 --> 02:22.639
start to just sort of trust it. It's like, ah, that's going to be working. It's, it looks great.

02:22.639 --> 02:27.680
If you do that, your, your defenses come down, you let your guard down and you go several steps

02:27.680 --> 02:33.439
forwards. And only then you realize that, that several steps ago, it made some terrible assumption

02:33.439 --> 02:38.560
that is complete nonsense. And as a result, you've just wasted lots of time.

02:38.560 --> 02:43.800
So, you know, the onus is on you to be challenging at each step.

02:43.800 --> 02:47.080
If there's, if you're debugging something and it declares it's found the problem and

02:47.080 --> 02:52.719
fixed it and here's why with lots of confident bold text, still challenge.

02:52.719 --> 02:54.360
Come in, demand the evidence.

02:54.360 --> 02:56.080
Have you found the root cause?

02:56.080 --> 02:57.240
Have you fixed it?

02:57.240 --> 02:58.680
Are you sure?

02:58.680 --> 03:00.520
Give me the evidence, demonstrate it,

03:00.520 --> 03:03.479
show me the test results, and then move on.

03:03.479 --> 03:05.840
But don't allow your guard to come down.

03:05.840 --> 03:08.960
Don't just go with it, even when you have some successes.

03:08.960 --> 03:11.039
One thing's for sure, you're gonna have moments

03:11.039 --> 03:14.240
when you will be blown away by how well it goes,

03:14.240 --> 03:15.900
how much code is built,

03:15.900 --> 03:17.199
how something like the camper

03:17.199 --> 03:18.960
and disappears that is really cool.

03:18.960 --> 03:21.400
It's gonna be amazing.

03:21.400 --> 03:25.360
But there will also be times when it's a fiasco.

03:25.360 --> 03:29.680
There will be chaos. There will be times when it makes a mistake and then it makes

03:29.680 --> 03:35.159
the same mistake again and you put it in agents.md and you say never, ever make this

03:35.159 --> 03:40.560
mistake and it makes it again. And you say write a performance report that says I will

03:40.560 --> 03:44.680
never make this mistake again and read that performance report every single time.

03:44.680 --> 03:46.280
And it still moves on the stage.

03:46.280 --> 03:50.080
When these things happen, it can be so frustrating.

03:50.080 --> 03:54.759
I've had moments when I have been just so angry.

03:54.759 --> 03:57.080
And what you have to keep telling yourself

03:57.080 --> 03:58.680
and what you gradually learn is that

03:58.680 --> 04:00.800
that is part of the process.

04:00.800 --> 04:02.919
You're hitting up against limitations

04:02.919 --> 04:04.639
of the model that you're working with.

04:04.639 --> 04:06.479
And you need to find how to work around that,

04:06.479 --> 04:12.879
you need to detect it, manage it, handle it and do so with style. This is the frustration,

04:12.879 --> 04:18.720
the walls that you can hit against, that is part of the process. These models give you an enormous

04:18.720 --> 04:24.240
leg up in some ways, but they also have their downsides and managing the downsides is the key

04:24.240 --> 04:30.720
path to success. Now, you might be thinking to yourself, these five principles to set me up for success,

04:30.720 --> 04:32.800
they seem to fly in the face of Yolo.

04:32.800 --> 04:34.600
It's very much about not just,

04:34.600 --> 04:35.839
"Hey, I'm gonna kick it off.

04:35.839 --> 04:37.319
"I'm gonna let it have permission.

04:37.319 --> 04:38.199
"Do whatever you want.

04:38.199 --> 04:40.959
"I'm coming back in an hour or in a day."

04:40.959 --> 04:42.439
That seems a bit different.

04:42.439 --> 04:43.879
And you're right.

04:43.879 --> 04:45.120
And you know, you could probably tell,

04:45.120 --> 04:47.079
"I'm not a huge fan of Yolo.

04:47.079 --> 04:48.959
"There are situations as we'll discuss

04:48.959 --> 04:50.959
"when it makes sense to do it,

04:50.959 --> 04:53.959
but always with a skeptical eye,

04:53.959 --> 04:56.120
always be the boss as much as you can.

04:56.120 --> 04:59.879
Even if you YOLO, come back and check everything carefully.

04:59.879 --> 05:02.079
People talk about the trust but verify mindset

05:02.079 --> 05:03.879
that I think I mentioned before.

05:03.879 --> 05:05.639
You need to trust it to a certain extent

05:05.639 --> 05:07.639
and then verify very carefully indeed.

05:07.639 --> 05:10.199
That is certainly my recipe for success.

05:10.199 --> 05:12.360
Even with the really strong models,

05:12.360 --> 05:14.199
you need to be the boss.

05:14.199 --> 05:15.800
That's the path to success.

05:15.800 --> 05:19.040
And on that note, I got some words of wisdom

05:19.040 --> 05:21.800
for whether you're from a junior background

05:21.800 --> 05:24.480
or from a senior engineer background.

05:24.480 --> 05:25.959
I just have some things to say.

05:25.959 --> 05:29.459
If you're from coming into this as like new to programming

05:29.459 --> 05:33.720
or a junior engineer on that end of the continuum,

05:33.720 --> 05:37.199
then first up, you need to use this as an opportunity

05:37.199 --> 05:38.399
to be learning.

05:38.399 --> 05:39.959
You want to take that journey yourself

05:39.959 --> 05:43.079
so that you can become a senior engineer in the future.

05:43.079 --> 05:46.800
And so, don't allow yourself to lose sight

05:46.800 --> 05:48.800
of the learning opportunity here.

05:48.800 --> 05:51.959
Take this as a way to challenge and ask questions

05:51.959 --> 05:54.240
of the LLM in order that you can understand

05:54.240 --> 05:56.139
what's happening so that you're learning it too.

05:56.139 --> 05:58.860
So you're making that journey towards being a senior engineer.

05:58.860 --> 06:01.399
Otherwise it would be so easy just to sort of

06:01.399 --> 06:05.180
not ever acquire that skillset and just let the model do,

06:05.180 --> 06:07.980
do the work, which will only lead to frustration later.

06:07.980 --> 06:11.439
And similarly, you need to be skeptical.

06:11.439 --> 06:13.639
The mental model to have is that you've got this,

06:13.639 --> 06:16.699
this assistant that's super eager

06:16.699 --> 06:19.920
and that will go and hunt on the internet and research things and do

06:19.920 --> 06:24.560
stuff. But sometimes it's misguided and sometimes it's overconfident and makes a

06:24.560 --> 06:30.319
mistake or it uses band aids instead of doing a proper solution. It guesses a lot all those

06:30.319 --> 06:36.160
kinds of problems that we've talked about in the past and you have to be wise to that.

06:36.160 --> 06:40.120
So come at it with a healthy skepticism, ask the right questions,

06:40.120 --> 06:42.720
always double check, even if it seems confident,

06:42.720 --> 06:45.600
always have that healthy skepticism in mind

06:45.600 --> 06:47.639
and things will go much better.

06:47.639 --> 06:50.959
And for senior engineers, to be honest,

06:50.959 --> 06:54.600
this tooling is best suited for you.

06:54.600 --> 06:56.920
It's harder for the junior engineers.

06:56.920 --> 06:58.639
Sorry, junior engineers.

06:58.639 --> 07:02.560
It's harder because you just sort of can feel bamboozled by this thing sometimes.

07:02.560 --> 07:04.680
And you can't tell if it's making it up or not.

07:04.680 --> 07:06.839
But as a senior engineer, you can tell.

07:06.839 --> 07:10.480
And so you're actually in a really good position to use it.

07:10.480 --> 07:17.040
The point I want to make to you is you need to keep in mind that this is a tool that allows

07:17.040 --> 07:20.079
you to build more when used correctly.

07:20.079 --> 07:22.360
I don't like to get caught up in the hype.

07:22.360 --> 07:26.240
I don't know that it's a 10x, but it's certainly something that allows you to do more

07:26.240 --> 07:27.360
and build more.

07:27.360 --> 07:33.319
And so my, my, my ask, my appeal is that you keep enjoying what you do.

07:33.319 --> 07:37.160
What excites most of us engineers is the fact of building.

07:37.160 --> 07:40.959
And sure, a lot of what gives me satisfaction is hammering out code and seeing it run.

07:40.959 --> 07:42.360
And so there's a sense when I work with L.A.

07:42.360 --> 07:45.600
Lens that some of that joy is being taken away from me.

07:45.600 --> 07:47.040
But really it's a trade.

07:47.040 --> 07:50.639
It's true that joy is being taken away of writing a line of code.

07:50.639 --> 07:53.240
But instead of that, I have this other joy that I

07:53.240 --> 07:56.519
am able to build entire systems like frontend

07:56.519 --> 07:57.920
that I'm so terrible at.

07:57.920 --> 08:01.000
Now I can just have a frontend built just like that.

08:01.000 --> 08:03.920
I can have terraformed scripts written and things deployed,

08:03.920 --> 08:06.240
DevOps stuff that I'm on.

08:06.240 --> 08:09.160
And that can be done really, really fast with LLMs.

08:09.160 --> 08:11.720
And so it's given me all these extra powers

08:11.720 --> 08:15.600
I didn't have before, and that allows me to build faster,

08:15.600 --> 08:18.120
and building faster is fun.

08:18.120 --> 08:22.759
So I guess my ask is to try and keep in mind

08:22.759 --> 08:24.680
that whilst it's taken something's away,

08:24.680 --> 08:26.639
it's given a lot more back.

08:26.639 --> 08:29.399
And in summary, I have a balanced view

08:29.399 --> 08:32.240
when it comes to the productivity improvement that

08:32.240 --> 08:33.879
coding agents bring us.

08:33.879 --> 08:38.740
I do see that there are situations when it's literally a 10x change in productivity,

08:38.740 --> 08:42.360
like the Building a Camban view in the next AS app.

08:42.360 --> 08:47.240
That would take me days, maybe even a week, whereas it was just a matter of minutes.

08:47.240 --> 08:50.860
So there are clearly situations where it is a game changer.

08:50.860 --> 08:55.559
But there are situations where that's definitely not the case. There are times when it's just an incremental

08:55.559 --> 08:59.519
productivity improvement, particularly with larger projects, legacy projects,

08:59.519 --> 09:04.320
with complex back-end changes. And there are situations where it actually

09:04.320 --> 09:09.159
subtracts value. I have had real-world situations where I've been trying to build

09:09.159 --> 09:17.200
like a streamable HTTP/MCP server, something that's relatively new and wasn't part of its training data and

09:17.200 --> 09:22.639
Claude was messing it up for me again and again and it took me longer than it would have taken if

09:22.639 --> 09:30.320
I just wrote it myself. So it's a mixed bag and recognizing that it's a mixed bag is a big part of

09:30.320 --> 09:35.120
the challenge. You could come into this, you could have a lot of success, you could think to

09:35.120 --> 09:36.080
yourself, wow, I can change

09:36.080 --> 09:39.679
my estimates to attend to what they were. And then you hit a stumbling block and the

09:39.679 --> 09:46.159
next thing you know, everything has been pushed out. So keep a healthy understanding

09:46.159 --> 09:50.720
of both the positives and the downsides of working with coding agents.

09:50.720 --> 09:56.039
And in aggregate, the main thing to take away is that I would say it's always a positive,

09:56.039 --> 10:01.080
it's always value add now. Using the top coding agents, always speeds things up.

10:01.080 --> 10:04.039
How much it speeds things up depends hugely

10:04.039 --> 10:06.440
on the kind of task, on the kinds of skills

10:06.440 --> 10:08.759
that you have, on the size of the project,

10:08.759 --> 10:10.279
and many other factors.

10:10.279 --> 10:12.159
But you quickly get to understand

10:12.159 --> 10:13.440
what kind of boost you're going to get

10:13.440 --> 10:15.320
once you're a little bit into that project.

10:15.320 --> 10:18.840
Generally speaking, if you're dealing with a small MVP

10:18.840 --> 10:21.159
of a green field project, you're going to get

10:21.159 --> 10:25.399
a very significant multiplier of your productivity.

10:25.399 --> 10:29.279
In most other cases, you're talking about a speed improvement, you're talking about

10:29.279 --> 10:35.240
assistance, but not the kind of 10X numbers that you sometimes see in all of the hype.

